{{Main|1992 United States Senate election in New York}}
{{See also|List of United States senators from New York}}

Incumbent Republican [[Al D'Amato]] won re-election to a third term over Democrat [[Robert Abrams]], [[New York State Attorney General]] and former [[Borough president]] of the [[Bronx]]. {{As of|2023}}, this was the last Senate election in New York won by a Republican.

Early in the campaign, environmentalist attorney, [[Laurance S. Rockefeller, Jr.]] nephew of the former governor [[Nelson Rockefeller|Nelson]], tried to challenge D'Amato in the Republican primary,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Purdum |first=Todd S. |date=July 12, 1992 |title=JULY 5-11: Rockefeller vs. D'Amato; A Powerful Political Name Reappears in New York |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/07/12/weekinreview/july-5-11-rockefeller-vs-d-amato-a-powerful-political-name-reappears-in-new-york.html}}</ref> but fell short of the required signatures to get onto the primary ballot. D'Amato summarily went unchallenged.

The Democratic primary campaign featured State Attorney General Robert Abrams, former U.S. Congresswoman and 1984 vice presidential candidate [[Geraldine Ferraro]], Reverend [[Al Sharpton]], Congressman [[Robert J. Mrazek]], and New York City Comptroller and former Congresswoman [[Elizabeth Holtzman]].  Abrams was considered the early front-runner.<ref name="nyt-running">{{Cite news |last=Kolbert, Elizabeth |date=October 21, 1991 |title=In Senate Campaign, Ferraro Picks Up Where She Left Off |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9D0CE5DE1E30F932A15753C1A967958260}}</ref>  Ferraro emphasized her career as a teacher, prosecutor, congresswoman, and mother, and talked about how she was tough on crime.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Braden |first=Maria |title=Women Politicians and the Media |publisher=[[The University Press of Kentucky]] |year=1996 |isbn=0-8131-1970-7 |location=Lexington, Kentucky |page=135}}</ref>  Ferraro drew attacks from the media and her opponents over her husband [[John Zaccaro]]'s finances and business relationships.<ref name="nyt-front">{{Cite news |last=Mitchell, Alison |date=September 1, 1992 |title=For Ferraro, Cheers of '84 Are Still Resonating |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9E0CE6DD1239F932A3575AC0A964958260}}</ref>

Ferraro became the front-runner, capitalizing on her star power from 1984 and using the campaign attacks against her as an explicitly feminist rallying point for women voters.<ref name="nyt-front" />  As the primary date neared, her lead began to dwindle under the charges, and she released additional tax returns from the 1980s to try to defray the attacks.<ref name="nyt091192tx">{{Cite news |last=Blumenthal, Ralph |date=September 11, 1992 |title=Ferraro Releases Tax Returns for 2 Missing Years to Offset Attacks by Rivals |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9E0CE7DD173DF932A2575AC0A964958260}}</ref> Holtzman ran a [[negative ad]] accusing Ferraro and Zaccaro of taking more than $300,000 in rent in the 1980s from a pornographer with purported ties to organized crime.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mitchell, Alison |date=August 27, 1992 |title=Holtzman Draws Criticism From Feminists Over Ads |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/08/27/nyregion/holtzman-draws-criticism-from-feminists-over-ads.html}}</ref>  The final debates were nasty, and Holtzman in particular constantly attacked Ferraro's integrity and finances.<ref name="pothole-465">{{Cite book |last=Lurie |first=Leonard |url=https://archive.org/details/senatorpotholeun00luri/page/465 |title=Senator Pothole: The Unauthorized Biography of Al D'Amato |publisher=[[Birch Lane Press]] |year=1994 |isbn=1-55972-227-4 |pages=[https://archive.org/details/senatorpotholeun00luri/page/465 465, 467] |url-access=registration}}</ref><ref name="nyt-abrams" />  In an unusual election-eve television broadcast, Ferraro talked about the ethnic slurs made against her as an Italian-American.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Purdum, Todd S. |author-link=Todd Purdum |date=September 15, 1992 |title=Senate Race Ends in Whirl Of Appeals |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/09/15/nyregion/the-1992-campaign-senate-race-ends-in-whirl-of-appeals.html}}</ref>  In the September 15, 1992 primary, Abrams edged out Ferraro by less than percentage point, winning 37&nbsp;percent of the vote to 36&nbsp;percent.<ref name="nyt-abrams">{{Cite news |last=Purdum, Todd S. |author-link=Todd Purdum |date=September 16, 1992 |title=Abrams, In Tight Senate Vote, Appears to Edge Out Ferraro |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9E0CE6DC1E31F935A2575AC0A964958260}}</ref>  Ferraro did not concede she had lost for two weeks.<ref name="nyt100192">{{Cite news |last=Verhovek, Sam Howe |date=October 1, 1992 |title=Abrams Gets A Concession From Ferraro |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/10/01/nyregion/abrams-gets-a-concession-from-ferraro.html}}</ref>

After Abrams emerged as the nominee, the Democrats remained divided.  In particular, Abrams spent much of the remainder of the campaign trying to get Ferraro's endorsement.<ref name="nyt-endor" /> Ferraro, enraged and bitter after the nature of the primary,<ref name="pothole-465" /><ref name="nyt100192" /> ignored Abrams and accepted [[Bill Clinton]]'s request to campaign for [[Bill Clinton presidential campaign, 1992|his presidential bid]] instead.  She was eventually persuaded by state party leaders into giving an unenthusiastic endorsement with just three days to go before the general election, in exchange for an apology by Abrams for the tone of the primary.<ref name="nyt-endor">{{Cite news |last=Manegold, Catherine S. |date=November 1, 1992 |title=Ferraro Gets An Apology From Abrams |work=[[The New York Times]] |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/11/01/nyregion/ferraro-gets-an-apology-from-abrams.html}}</ref>

Abrams was also criticized for calling D'Amato a [[fascist]], and he narrowly lost the general election as a result of these controversies.<ref>[https://query.nytimes.com/gst/fullpage.html?res=9F0CE1DB1138F93AA3575AC0A965958260 Attorney General Abrams to Quit To Join a Law Firm in Manhattan]. New York Times. September 9, 1993.</ref>

