Incumbent Republican [[Bob Packwood]] won re-election to his fifth term.

As the election season got underway, analysts from both major parties predicted that Packwood would have one of the toughest seats to defend in what was anticipated to be a volatile election year.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ulrich |first=Roberta |date=December 21, 1991 |title=Demos, GOP look to the West for vote gains |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Packwood was regarded as one of the nation's "most powerful elected officials"<ref>{{Cite news |date=September 16, 2009 |title=Bob Packwood |work=Willamette Week}}</ref> with "extraordinary political instincts."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Egan |first=Timothy |date=September 9, 1995 |title=Packwood Is Leaving As a Pariah In His State |work=The New York Times}}</ref> But the state's largest newspaper, ''The Oregonian'', had described AuCoin (Packwood's presumed main challenger) as having "persistence, imagination and clout [that] have made him the most powerful congressman in Oregon and one of the most influential members from the Northwest."<ref>''The Oregonian'', June 13, 1988.</ref>

For AuCoin, however, first came the Democratic primary. He faced Portland attorney Joe Wetzel and [[Bend, Oregon|Bend]] businessman [[Harry Lonsdale]] in what became a "brutal, bitter"<ref name="SeaTi51992">{{Cite news |last=Matassa |first=Mark |date=May 18, 1992 |title=Great political lineup in Oregon primary, but it's not the NBA – is voters' mood a pregame show for Washington? |work=The Seattle Times}}</ref> contest.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=December 31, 1991 |title=Senate aspirant proposes restoring tax deductions |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Lonsdale, who had run a close race against incumbent [[Mark Hatfield]] for Oregon's other Senate seat [[1990 United States Senate election in Oregon|in 1990]], emerged as AuCoin's principal rival; Wetzel, who criticized Packwood and AuCoin as long-term, ineffective members of Congress,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hortsch |first=Dan |date=January 30, 1992 |title=U.S. Senate candidate urges tax law reforms |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> trailed throughout the race, and was not invited to an April debate sponsored by the [[City Club of Portland]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Duin |first=Steve |date=January 28, 1992 |title=No debate for Wetzel? Inconceivable! |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Lonsdale took on "the Les AuCoin-Mark Hatfield-Bob Packwood coalition" as his primary cause, stating "I consider Les AuCoin a good man who has been corrupted by PAC money over the years".<ref>{{Cite news |last=Duin |first=Steve |date=September 19, 1991 |title=The return of a career candidate |pages=B07 |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

In a race the ''Seattle Times'' called "as negative as many voters can remember,"<ref name=SeaTi51992/> Lonsdale attacked AuCoin as "corrupt"<ref name=SeaTi51992/> and tied to the timber industry.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=February 9, 1992 |title=Demo Senate primary gets rough |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Lonsdale's environmental credentials also came under scrutiny,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Walth |first=Brent |date=March 21, 1992 |title=Lonsdale Firm's Hazardous Waste Violated No Rules |work=The Register – Guard – Eugene, Or. |url=https://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/registerguard/access/6606344.html?dids=6606344:6606344&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121023081627/http://pqasb.pqarchiver.com/registerguard/access/6606344.html?dids=6606344:6606344&FMT=ABS&FMTS=ABS:FT |url-status=dead |archive-date=October 23, 2012 |access-date=October 8, 2009}}</ref> and AuCoin noted Lonsdale's reversal of support for nuclear power and belated opposition to the re-opening of [[Trojan Nuclear Power Plant]].<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=January 5, 1992 |title=Lonsdale, in about-face, opposes nuclear power, Trojan restart |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> AuCoin turned accusations of undue influence back on Lonsdale, pointing out that his company (Bend Research) had received millions in federal defense contracts.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=March 29, 1992 |title=AuCoin takes Lonsdale's role in debate |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

Even during the primary, Packwood and AuCoin traded barbs on various issues.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=February 18, 1992 |title=Packwood, AuCoin exchange accusations |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Packwood joined Lonsdale in criticizing AuCoin for his involvement in what was reported as a rash of check-bouncing among members of Congress; AuCoin characterized the issue as a series of mistakes, rather than gross abuses.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Ota |first1=Alan K. |last2=Roberta Ulrich |date=March 14, 1992 |title=Oregonians check books |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> In what was believed to be an unprecedented move, Packwood attempted to influence the Democratic primary's outcome by running television ads against AuCoin.<ref name="nyt92">{{Cite news |date=May 24, 1992 |title=The 1992 Campaign; Close Vote for Oregon Senate Seat Insures Recount |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1992/05/24/us/the-1992-campaign-close-vote-for-oregon-senate-seat-insures-recount.html |access-date=October 8, 2009}}</ref>

Ultimately, the results of the Democratic primary were so close that an automatic recount was triggered.<ref name=nyt92/> AuCoin held a news conference on May 23 in the [[South Park Blocks]] stating he would wait for the recount, but the margin was currently 248 votes in his favor.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hamilton |first=Don |date=May 24, 1992 |title=Aucoin waits for official declaration of victory |pages=D05 |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> On June 18, over a month after the primary election, AuCoin was certified as having won by 330 votes.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=June 18, 1992 |title=State puts its seal on AuCoin's victory |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> Upon conceding the race, Lonsdale pondered mounting a write-in campaign, reiterating that Oregon needed an "outsider" in the Senate.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=June 9, 1992 |title=A recount in the Democratic Senate primary is… |work=The Oregonian}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=June 19, 1992 |title=Lonsdale concedes primary loss with attack on AuCoin, Packwood |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

