By the end of June, when the recount was complete, AuCoin was nearly out of campaign funds; Packwood entered the general election race with $3.2 million<ref>{{Cite news |date=June 26, 1992 |title=Let's make a deal |work=The Oregonian}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=June 23, 1992 |title=Packwood rejects AuCoin's spending-lid plan |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> and was ranked sixth nationwide among senators raising funds outside their home state during the 1990–1992 election season.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ota |first=Alan K. |date=July 2, 1992 |title=Packwood ranked sixth in Senate in raising money outside of state |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

AuCoin opposed weakening the Endangered Species Act (ESA) to erase the Northern Spotted Owl's impact on the timber industry, but Packwood (“one of the timber industry’s chief allies,” according to Oregon State University political scientist [[William Lunch]]<ref>{{Cite news |last=Tumulty |first=Karen |date=November 3, 1993 |title=Catching a 'Chameleon': Senate Wrestles With Packwood |work=Los Angeles Times |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1993-11-03-mn-52686-story.html}}</ref>) assailed “environmental extremists” and introduced legislation to convene a presidential cabinet committee to exempt the endangered owl from the ESA.<ref>{{Cite news |date=October 18, 1990 |title=Packwood Wants Changes In Endangered Species Act |work=Spokane Chronicle |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?nid=1345&dat=19901018&id=EssSAAAAIBAJ&pg=6800,2600184 }}{{Dead link|date=August 2024 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref>

In September, Packwood pulled ads that had falsely criticized AuCoin for missing votes while speaking to special interest groups.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=September 26, 1992 |title=Inaccuracy found |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> By October, Packwood had raised $8 million,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Ota |first=Alak K. |date=October 30, 1992 |title=Data sparse on Packwood's donors |work=The Oregonian}}</ref> spending $5.4 million more than AuCoin, and leading all Senate incumbents.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Hamilton |first=Don |date=May 25, 1993 |title=Packwood sets '92 campaign spending record |work=The Oregonian |url=http://docs.newsbank.com/openurl?ctx_ver=z39.88-2004&rft_id=info:sid/iw.newsbank.com:NewsBank:ORGB&rft_val_format=info:ofi/fmt:kev:mtx:ctx&rft_dat=0EB087CBCE407D62&svc_dat=InfoWeb:aggregated5&req_dat=0D10F2CADB4B24C0 |access-date=October 8, 2009}}</ref> Yet that fall, the two candidates were in a dead heat, with Packwood continuing to criticize AuCoin on attendance, his House bank account and the spotted owl, and AuCoin echoing the campaign of popular Presidential candidate [[Bill Clinton]] by accusing Packwood of favoring the wealthy over the middle class.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=October 29, 1992 |title=Packwood, AuCoin in dead heat, new poll finds |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

The outcome of the bruising race was too close to call on election night, but on the following day, Packwood emerged as the winner with about 52% of the vote to AuCoin's 47. In his victory press conference, Packwood endorsed AuCoin for [[United States Secretary of the Interior|Secretary of the Interior]] in the [[Clinton administration]].<ref>{{Cite news |date=November 5, 1992 |title=Sen. Packwood Backs Foe For Cabinet |work=The San Francisco Chronicle |agency=Associated Press}}</ref><ref name="cain">{{Cite news |last=Cain |first=Brad |date=November 5, 1992 |title=Packwood: defeated foe would be good Interior chief |work=[[The Bulletin (Bend)]] |url=https://news.google.com/newspapers?id=1hcpAAAAIBAJ&pg=7093,1262600 |access-date=April 10, 2010}}</ref> When told of Packwood's comments, AuCoin responded by saying "I think that's real special."<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mapes |first=Jeff |date=November 5, 1992 |title=Victorious Packwood boosts foe for cabinet |work=The Oregonian}}</ref>

